  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Antrodia heteromorpha is a wood-decaying bracket fungus in the Polypore family, characterized by its small, thin fruiting bodies and variable pore structure. This species is found on dead hardwood in northeastern North America and plays an important ecological role in forest decomposition.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Variable Pore Structure",
      "description": "Pores are notably irregular and angular, varying in size across the same fruiting body, which is the most distinctive feature of this species",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small, Thin Fruiting Bodies",
      "description": "Develops small bracket-like fruiting bodies, typically less than 5 cm wide, that are relatively thin and brittle when dry",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Substrate Preference",
      "description": "Found on dead hardwood logs and branches, with a cream to pale tan colored cap surface",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Antrodia heteromorpha grows on dead hardwood, particularly fallen logs and branches in moist forests throughout New England.",
